{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 asserts an unconditional constant limit but its mechanism is explicitly conditional: it says 'If the number of stable matchings of small size is Poisson(λ) and their basins have total probability bounded away from 0 and 1...' It never proves these conditions for random 3-regular graphs, nor derives the claimed Θ(1) expected number of reachable obstructions. Thus the central quantitative law is assumed rather than shown, and the answer does not establish that the limit is strictly between 0 and 1.","target":"1191498e4fe524d7c1f843e8566c7a215d8d53f8b5b9922d17bfb1f789ae4fe2"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's obstruction characterization is too restrictive. Local stability on a 3-regular graph only requires each vertex to have at most one opposite-color neighbor, i.e. crossing edges form a matching. A vertex with zero crossing edges has all three neighbors in its own color class, so its color class has degree-3 vertices and is not a disjoint union of cycles. Thus the claimed 'each color class is a disjoint union of cycles' excludes valid stable mixed configurations, so the characterization is false.","target":"e1b1108a5b225e9fccc6a5e6bb42303519c6cc10405d88839438b56b976c35f5"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_003's quantitative claim that stable mixed configurations are 'exponentially rare, with expected number exp(-c n)' is false. For any matching M of size αn, coloring each component of G-M monochromatically gives a stable cut whose crossing edges are exactly M. The expected number of such matchings in a random 3-regular graph is exp(Θ(n)) for suitable α<1/2, and the component coloring contributes only a polynomial factor when α<1/2. Hence the expected number of stable mixed configurations is exponentially large, not exp(-c n).","target":"f8d3b64a774e0d1e4097668829b150ca3dbfaba0dcf2c3648fb197898c372e66"}]}